commodity prices are hot right now and  prices are surging but the impact of  these price increases on our everyday  lives is a bit more tenuous than most  people realize when the price of things  like arabica beans rise you might assume  that's going to bump up what you pay for  a cup of coffee from your local barista  but the price of coffee at the coffee  shop actually has little to do with the  price of the underlying commodity some  commodity prices do drive the rate  consumers pay but for others the  correlation is pretty small i'm going to  highlight a few examples and i'll  explain what you should really be  watching to tell you the direction of  consumer prices  with some commodities when market prices  rise that's going to move prices at the  store lumber is a great example when  prices rise in the lumber market you see  it pretty immediately on the shelves  lowe's famously installed digital price  signs in its lumber aisles so the  company wouldn't have to constantly  change them during the day with lumber  what's traded is mostly purchased by  consumers rather than broken up and  financialized through contracts on  exchanges propane is another example  prices for home tanks and cylinders move  largely in line with commodity prices  the price of oil is pretty tied to  gasoline and diesel prices but other  factors like taxes and labor costs do  play a role but with typical everyday  products it takes time to convert the  commodity into the retail goods we buy  and manufacturers often lock in prices  months in advance that's true for  ingredients such as corn for tortilla  chips or wheat flour for bread or the  cotton that goes into clothing the price  you pay for a cup of coffee at starbucks  is really more about the paper board  that's used to make the cups those  prices have jumped recently and that's a  bigger reason for the higher prices  you're seeing at the coffee shop than  the coffee beans themselves and the  rising cost of wheat won't affect beer  prices right away much of that cost  comes from aluminum cans and the cost of  energy and shipping if you're trying to  get a grip on which commodities really  move the needle here are a few you  should watch  the first is corn corn is in just about  everything because it's the chief  ingredient in the high fructose corn  syrup that's in products ranging from  bread to barbecue sauce and mini juices  and sodas corn is also a major input for  ethanol which is used in gasoline so  higher corn prices often mean higher gas  prices you should also watch oil higher  gas and diesel prices make it more  expensive to transport products and to  refine things like plastics  last but certainly not least is copper  known on markets as dr copper it's long  been an indicator for the overall health  of the economy because it's used in just  about everything housing electrical  equipment even motors copper prices for  home builders exporters and  manufacturers are generally very tied to  the price on commodity markets so when  you see copper prices going up that's  generally a good sign that consumer  demand is rising and we're seeing a  strong economy  rising commodity prices are playing a  role in the high level of inflation  we're seeing but maybe not in the places  people normally think of if you're  watching the market for signs that your  favorite products are going to be  getting more expensive  make sure you're looking in the right  places if you enjoyed this video hit the  like button and subscribe to keep up  with my latest videos on markets and the  economy i'm reminded of college park's  own gunna who says my new condominium is  dressed i see vvss and baguettes imma  get these commas and invest  i'll see you next time
 

